  "school": "SMC",
  "asset": "EURUSD",
  "timeframe": "1H",
  "market_context": "Imperfect uptrend with choppy price action; analyst focuses on distinguishing major swing lows (origin of valid BOS) from minor swings to identify true market structure and avoid false reversals.",
  "key_observation": "A swing point is only a major swing after a valid break of structure occurs; additional criteria—fair value gap (imbalance) and sufficient price distance (≥50% of prior impulse)—determine its strength (1–3 stars).",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: The analyst states that a swing low cannot be labeled 'major' until a valid break of structure (BOS) occurs above it; only then does the origin become a major swing low.",
    "Step 2: The analyst introduces the second criterion: presence of a fair value gap (FVG/imbalance) at the swing point, which strengthens its significance as a major swing.",
    "Step 3: The analyst adds the third criterion: the impulsive move originating from the swing must push price at least half the distance of the prior impulse; insufficient distance invalidates it as a major swing.",
    "Step 4: The analyst presents three schematic examples: (1) no BOS, no FVG, no distance → minor swing; (2) BOS + distance but no FVG → major swing (2 stars); (3) BOS + FVG + sufficient distance → major swing (3 stars).",
    "Step 5: The analyst notes two exceptions that invalidate a change of character even when breaking a major swing: liquidity sweep (stop hunt in key liquidity zone) and unmitigated FVG below the swing."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Major swings require three criteria: valid BOS, imbalance (FVG), and sufficient impulse distance. Breaking a minor swing doesn’t signal reversal; only breaking a confirmed major swing (with all criteria) indicates true change of character—unless invalidated by liquidity sweep or FVG mitigation.",
